When one ARC task supplies examples with superficially similar separators or lattices but markedly different layouts, don't infer that every pair uses the same encoding merely from appearance. For each pair, normalize structural roles and describe the actual input-to-output mapping, including output shape and what information is preserved or discarded. Maintain an explicit coverage table: candidate mechanism × demo, with exact match, counterevidence, or untested. A candidate must explain every complete pair, not just the nearest-looking example.

After a rejection, treat the submitted prediction as contradicted; don't resubmit it unchanged just because the demo budget is exhausted. If remaining attempts exist but no single coherent rule has passed all demos, weigh another submission as a genuine uncertainty gamble rather than calling it verified. When implementation/fixture checks are incomplete, report the limitation internally and avoid equating successful program execution with behavioral validation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-workflow/references/heterogeneous-rule-evidence.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis-discipline", "file": "references/request-then-synthesize.md", "content": "# Request-then-synthesize discipline

For each new demonstration, compare its complete input and output against prior pairs before requesting another. Record changed cells/colors and notable unchanged structures, then list remaining rule candidates and their predictions on the test. A request is warranted only when a stated ambiguity can change the implementation or test output and the next pair could distinguish it. After receipt, decide explicitly whether the candidate set changed; if not, re-evaluate the request strategy rather than repeating the same broad exploration. Several syntactically valid tool calls or successful test-only executions are not evidence that a shared rule has been found. Require one candidate to explain every pair, including apparently unrelated geometries, before submission; preserve uncertainty if that criterion remains unmet.

In the reviewed stream, multiple heterogeneous demos were gathered and analyzed, but no exact cross-demo transform was implemented or fixture-verified before successive requests. The reusable lesson is to make every request hypothesis-driven and to keep synthesis and exact regression—not demo count—as the progress measure.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-synthesis-discipline/references/request-then-synthesize.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis-discipline", "file": "references/session-heuristics.md", "content": "## Session heuristic: repeated demo requests without testable hypotheses

When demo outputs use different palettes, orientations, and grid sizes, do not react to each pair with a fresh speculative identity or marker story. Record complete coordinate diffs and normalize salient structures (edge guide, interior markers, filled spans) to separate selection from operation. Each next request should discriminate a stated pair of candidate rules. A demo request that does not change the candidate set is likely wasted effort.

A pre-existing task file may be a placeholder rather than a verified transform. Inspect and execute it as protocol requires, but do not let its existence anchor later reasoning. Preserve a functional candidate while exploring; do not replace it with no-op code. Behavioral verification means exact full-grid reproduction of every supplied pair, not successful import, lint, or execution on the test alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-synthesis-discipline/references/session-heuristics.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-verdict-recovery", "file": "references/rejected-hypothesis-action-selection.md", "content": "# Post-rejection next-action discipline

In a continuing ARC instance, a rejection is evidence against the candidate rule, not evidence for any particular alternative. Preserve the exact test and attempt/demo counters. If no complete demo pairs are available to distinguish alternatives, request one demonstration rather than editing the program into an unsupported identity/no-op or other placeholder. Only revise the transform after extracting the complete pair's coordinate/color differences; then regression-test all pairs and execute the task file on the test before any resubmission.

A failed Python import or harness invocation is incidental. Correct the path/import method and continue; do not mistake the tooling error for puzzle evidence, and do not respond by writing an unverified transform.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790687614/attempts/9048647a014d435d8ae09727a7b7c5b6/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-verdict-recovery/references/rejected-hypothesis-action-selection.md"}
